<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clodoald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 522–c. 560</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Cloud · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clodoald, known as Saint Cloud, was a son of the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodomer">Chlodomer of Orléans</a>. When his uncles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> murdered his brothers in 524 to seize their inheritance, he escaped, later renounced his claim and became a monk. He founded a monastery at Novigentum near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he was buried, and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cloud">Saint-Cloud</a> is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clodoald.jpg" alt="Clodoald" width="270" height="596" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Daveswagon at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saintcloudstatue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Clodoald was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> and the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodomer">Chlodomer of Orléans</a> and his wife Guntheuc. After Chlodomer was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vezeronce">Battle of Vézeronce</a> on 25 June 524, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, Clodoald and his two brothers were raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by their grandmother, the queen dowager Clotilde. Their uncles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a>, king of Paris, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Clotaire I</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> wanted the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> for themselves.</p><p>By one account the uncles asked Clotilde to send them the boys so that they could be proclaimed their father's successors, and then had them killed; some say they killed the older two, Thibault and Gonthaire, aged ten and seven, with their own hands. Another account has them ask Clotilde whether the boys should be shorn of their long hair, the sign of Frankish nobility, or killed. Clodoald was saved by a few faithful followers and found refuge with Remigius, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Renouncing the throne, he became a disciple of the hermit Séverin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. At about twenty the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Paris cut his long hair, and his uncles, no longer seeing him as a threat, gave him some of his inheritance. After Séverin's death he lived for eleven years in Provence, where people came to him for healing, then returned to his first hermitage. Bishop Eusebius of Paris ordained him a priest in 551. Seeking to escape the honours that followed, he retired to Novigentum on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, two leagues below Paris, where he built a church dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Martin of Tours</a> and, according to tradition, a monastery endowed with his uncles' gifts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to legend he foretold his death, and miracles followed at his tomb, which drew crowds of pilgrims; Novigentum took the name Sanctus Clodoaldus in his honour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> wrote that he despised an earthly kingdom, cut his hair with his own hand and died a priest. His relics are kept in the Église Saint-Clodoald, and his feast day is 7 September. He is venerated in both the Catholic and Eastern Orthodox churches, and St. Cloud in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> are named after the French town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clodoald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The third son, <b>Clodoald</b>, escaped and became a monk; the town of Saint-Cloud near Paris is named after him.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-orleans">Kingdom of Orléans</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cloud">Saint-Cloud</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.85,2.22&amp;year=546" aria-label="Saint-Cloud on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Clodoald"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5125 5946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5125 5946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.85,2.22&amp;year=546">What was here in 546 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
